{# Document header: a two-column grid. Identity sits left, context right, and
   the byline spans both beneath them, so the width earns its keep instead of
   stacking five short lines down the page.

   Source order is identity, byline, context -- deliberately not the visual
   order. Grid placement puts context back up on row one, which lets the author
   follow the subtitle for a screen reader and for pdftotext without moving
   anything on screen. On a narrow viewport the columns collapse and the header
   stacks in this source order -- see _page-style.css.j2.

   `has-context`, `has-byline` and `has-dates` come from
   frontmatter-filter.lua, which is also what guarantees a blank key reads as
   an absent one here. #}
$if(title)$
<header class="doc-title">
    <div class="doc-identity">$title$
$if(subtitle)$
      <div class="doc-subtitle">$subtitle$</div>
$endif$
    </div>
$if(has-byline)$
  <div class="doc-byline">
$if(author)$
    {# Each name is its own element so a list wraps between names rather
       than mid-name, and the separator travels with the name it follows.
       author: takes a single value or a YAML list; a list is what a team
       project needs, and six names have to survive the header.

       The spaces here are real characters, not a flex gap. A gap paints
       the space but leaves nothing behind, so copy-paste and pdftotext
       both read "AuthorAda Lovelace". #}
    <div class="doc-meta"><span class="doc-label">Author</span> $for(author)$<span class="doc-author">$author$</span>$sep$ <span class="doc-author-sep" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span> $endfor$</div>
$endif$
$if(has-facts)$
    {# Issued, Due and Points on one line, spread across the space the author
       does not use. They were a stacked column and a badge beside the title;
       the badge read as a UI chip on a document that has no other chrome.

       Separators are their own elements rather than ::before content, so the
       flex row can space them evenly instead of hanging each one off the item
       that follows it. Each is conditional on something actually preceding
       it AND on the item it precedes existing -- a separator emitted on
       'something came before' alone strands one whenever the next fact is
       absent, which is the bug this shape prevents.

       The separator's spaces are non-breaking. It is its own flex item, so
       ordinary spaces collapse away at its edges and the header extracts
       as "Sep 01·Due" -- fine on screen, wrong in a paste and wrong in
       pdftotext.

       One line on purpose: a newline between these collapses to a word space
       that lands on one side of a separator only. #}
    <div class="doc-facts">$if(date-label)$<span class="doc-fact doc-issued"><span class="doc-label">Issued</span> <time datetime="$date-iso$">$date-label$</time></span>$endif$$if(due-label)$$if(sep-before-due)$<span class="doc-fact-sep" aria-hidden="true">&#160;&middot;&#160;</span>$endif$<span class="doc-fact doc-due"><span class="doc-label">Due</span> <time datetime="$due-iso$">$due-label$</time></span>$endif$$if(points-label)$$if(sep-before-points)$<span class="doc-fact-sep" aria-hidden="true">&#160;&middot;&#160;</span>$endif$<span class="doc-fact doc-points"><span class="doc-label">Points</span> $points-label$</span>$endif$</div>
$endif$
  </div>
$endif$
$if(has-context)$
  {# Written last, placed first. One line on purpose: a newline between these
     spans collapses to a word space that lands on only one side of the
     generated separator, which is asymmetric in a way no margin can correct. #}
  <div class="doc-context">$if(brand)$<div class="doc-brand$if(brand-is-image)$ doc-brand--image$endif$">$brand$</div>$endif$$if(program)$<span class="doc-program">$program$</span>$endif$$if(section)$<span class="doc-section">$section$</span>$endif$$if(term)$<span class="doc-term">$term$</span>$endif$</div>
$endif$
</header>
$endif$
$body$
